== where_to_stay ==

Where to Stay

Hotel choice shapes the entire trip. The decision is bigger than people realize, because where you sleep determines how you transport, when you arrive at the parks, what perks you get, what you pay for food, and how easy it is to take a midday break. The first cut is on-property versus off-property. The second cut, if you're going on-property, is which tier and which resort. This page walks through Disney on-property, off-property, and DVC. Pick whichever section applies and read that one first.

+ [On-Property at Disney] -> stay_disney_on_property
+ [Off-Property] -> stay_off_property
+ [DVC and Disney Deluxe Villas] -> stay_dvc
+ [Back to start] -> start

== stay_disney_on_property ==

On-Property at Disney

Disney runs over 25 resort hotels on its own property, ranging from budget motels to five-star villas. The reason to stay on-property is the perks. The reason not to is the price.

The perks, every Disney resort:

Early Theme Park Entry: 30 minutes in any Disney park before the general public, every day of your stay. This is the single most valuable perk. The first 30 minutes of the day at Magic Kingdom or Hollywood Studios are worth more than any other 30-minute window in the park.

Free Disney transportation: Buses to every park, monorail at the Magic Kingdom-area resorts, Skyliner at the EPCOT-area resorts, boats from select resorts. You can do an entire week without a car.

Free standard parking at the parks: If you do drive, you're not paying $35 a day to park.

MagicBand+ at a discount, package delivery from in-park stores back to your room, the My Disney Experience app integration for room keys and dining, and 60-day dining reservation booking instead of the 30-day window for off-property guests.

Extended Evening Hours, Deluxe and Deluxe Villa resorts only: Two additional hours at one park per week after the general public leaves. This is a Deluxe-tier perk and a major value play if you're considering the price jump from Moderate to Deluxe.

The catch: Disney resort prices are 30 to 60 percent higher than comparable off-property hotels in the same square-footage range. You're paying for the perks and the location.

+ [Continue to Disney Resort Tiers →] -> stay_disney_tiers
+ [Back to Where to Stay] -> where_to_stay

== stay_disney_tiers ==

Disney Resort Tiers

Disney sorts its resorts into four tiers. The tier you pick determines your nightly rate, your room size, your dining options, and whether you get Extended Evening Hours.

Value resorts: All-Star Movies, All-Star Music, All-Star Sports, Pop Century, Art of Animation. Smallest rooms, brightest theming, lowest prices. Bus transportation only, no monorail or Skyliner. Pop Century and Art of Animation have Skyliner access. Best for budget-conscious families and groups who want on-property perks at the lowest entry point. Art of Animation has family suites that sleep six, the best deal on Disney property for larger families.

Moderate resorts: Caribbean Beach, Coronado Springs, Port Orleans Riverside, Port Orleans French Quarter, Fort Wilderness Cabins. Bigger rooms, better theming, more grown-up vibe. Caribbean Beach has Skyliner access to EPCOT and Hollywood Studios. Port Orleans Riverside has the best value-per-dollar in this tier and the best theming.

Deluxe resorts: Contemporary, Polynesian, Grand Floridian, Wilderness Lodge, Animal Kingdom Lodge, Yacht Club, Beach Club, BoardWalk, Old Key West (technically a Villa resort with Deluxe-tier perks), Riviera. Walking distance to a park from Contemporary, Polynesian, Grand Floridian, BoardWalk, Yacht Club, and Beach Club. Extended Evening Hours included. Best dining on property. Significantly higher prices.

Deluxe Villa resorts: Bay Lake Tower, Polynesian Villas, Grand Floridian Villas, Boulder Ridge and Copper Creek at Wilderness Lodge, Animal Kingdom Lodge Villas (Jambo and Kidani), BoardWalk Villas, Beach Club Villas, BoardWalk's villas, Old Key West, Saratoga Springs, Riviera. Larger rooms, kitchens, washer/dryer in most layouts. These are DVC properties available to non-members through cash bookings or through point rentals. The DVC point rental option is covered in its own section.

Swan, Dolphin, Swan Reserve: Marriott-managed hotels on Disney property. Get most Disney perks (Early Entry, Disney transportation), do not get Extended Evening Hours. Often available through Marriott points or corporate rates that beat Disney's published prices.

+ [Continue to On-Property Tradeoffs →] -> stay_disney_tradeoffs
+ [Back to Where to Stay] -> where_to_stay

== stay_disney_tradeoffs ==

On-Property Tradeoffs

Pros: The perks are real and they save time. Early Entry alone often pays for the price premium if you value not waiting in 90-minute lines. Free transportation means no rental car cost. The Disney bubble is genuinely relaxing, kids can be themselves, you can drink without worrying about driving, and the immersion doesn't break when you go back to your room.

Cons: The price premium is also real. Food on Disney property is more expensive than off-property, and there are no easy off-site dinner options without leaving the bubble. The Disney transportation system is reliable but slow, especially the buses, and getting between two parks during a single day takes longer than it should. Resort hopping for dining is fun but eats hours.

Who should stay on-property: First-time visitors who want the full experience. Families with young kids who benefit from the midday break to a nearby room. Anyone going for a full week. Multi-generational groups where some members will skip park days and want to enjoy the resort.

Who should not: Couples doing a quick 3-day trip on a budget. Larger groups who'd save significant money in a vacation rental.

