== stay_dvc ==

DVC and Disney Deluxe Villas

Disney Vacation Club, DVC, is Disney's timeshare program. Members buy points up front and use them to book stays at DVC properties. You don't have to be a member to stay at a DVC resort. There are two ways in for non-members.

Path one, book DVC properties as a regular cash guest through Disney: This is the most expensive way to do it. Disney's published cash rates for the Villas are roughly equivalent to Deluxe resort rates plus a premium for the larger room. Studios sleep 4 or 5, one-bedrooms sleep 4 or 5 with a kitchen, two-bedrooms sleep 8 or 9, three-bedroom Grand Villas sleep 12. If you'd otherwise book two Deluxe rooms, a two-bedroom DVC villa is often cheaper, includes a full kitchen and washer/dryer, and you're all under one roof.

Path two, rent points from a DVC member: This is the cheaper option and most non-members don't know it exists. DVC members who can't use their points in a given year often rent them out, either directly through forums and brokers, or through dedicated rental services like David's Vacation Club Rentals and DVC Rental Store. The renter pays a per-point rate (currently in the $20 to $26 range depending on resort and broker). The total cost typically lands 30 to 50 percent below Disney's published cash rate for the same room.

What you get either way: Full Disney resort perks. Early Entry, free transportation, Extended Evening Hours at the Deluxe Villa resorts, MagicBand+, package delivery, all of it. The only thing you don't get with rented points is the ability to make changes or cancel close to your trip date, the rental contract is what it is.

The DVC resorts: Bay Lake Tower (Magic Kingdom monorail), Polynesian Villas (Magic Kingdom monorail), Grand Floridian Villas (Magic Kingdom monorail), Boulder Ridge and Copper Creek (boat to Magic Kingdom), Animal Kingdom Lodge Jambo and Kidani (savanna views), BoardWalk Villas (walk to EPCOT and Hollywood Studios), Beach Club Villas (walk to EPCOT), Riviera (Skyliner to EPCOT and Hollywood Studios), Old Key West (boat to Disney Springs), Saratoga Springs (boat to Disney Springs).

DVC member-only perks: DVC Member Lounges at multiple parks, including McKim's Mile House at Magic Kingdom. Renters do not get lounge access, those perks stay with the member.

+ [Continue to How to Rent DVC Points →] -> stay_dvc_renting
+ [Back to Where to Stay] -> where_to_stay

== stay_dvc_renting ==

How to Rent DVC Points

The point rental market is well-established. The two largest brokers are David's Vacation Club Rentals and DVC Rental Store, both in business for over a decade with thousands of completed bookings. There are also smaller brokers and a healthy direct-rental market on forums like the DIS Boards.

How it works through a broker: You tell the broker your dates, party size, and preferred resort. They match your request to a member willing to rent points. You pay the broker, the broker books the reservation in your name, you get a confirmation directly from Disney. The reservation is yours.

Pricing: Per-point rates vary by broker, resort, and season. Higher-demand resorts (Polynesian Villas, Grand Floridian Villas, Bay Lake Tower) and higher-demand seasons (holidays, summer) cost more. A typical one-week studio stay in moderate season runs roughly $1,800 to $3,000 depending on resort. The same week as a Disney cash booking would be $3,500 to $5,500.

Tradeoffs: Cancellations are difficult. Most rental contracts are non-refundable or refundable only with significant penalty. Travel insurance is more important when renting DVC points than when booking direct. Date flexibility is limited, you book a specific reservation and you keep it. Modifications are hard to come by.

Booking window: DVC bookings open 11 months in advance for members at their home resort, 7 months in advance at other resorts. Renters work within this window, which means popular resorts in high seasons (Polynesian at Christmas, BoardWalk in October during food and wine festival) need to be requested 7 to 11 months ahead. Off-season studios at less-trafficked resorts are often available 1 to 3 months out.

When DVC rental makes sense: Trips of 4 nights or more (the per-point math improves with longer stays), parties that would otherwise need a one-bedroom or larger, anyone who values having a kitchen and washer/dryer in their unit, and any traveler willing to commit firmly to dates 7+ months out in exchange for 30 to 50 percent savings on Deluxe-tier accommodations.

When it doesn't: Last-minute trips. Trips with high cancellation risk. Single-night stays.

